From: Anonymous Submission #60 <anon@example.invalid>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 12:58:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] DO NOT DEPLOY: export kallsyms_lookup_name() to modules

The synthetic primitive module (attack/primitives/kmod) resolves RNG symbols
such as base_crng, net_secret and table_perturb by name at load time, so the
case studies can be driven without hardcoding an offset per build.
kallsyms_lookup_name() was unexported in v5.7 precisely to stop out-of-tree
modules doing this.

This is research instrumentation rather than a re-introduced CVE: it is what
makes the synthetic primitive usable, and it is not required by the
CVE-2022-34918 path, which derives its offsets from vmlinux at build time.

Handing every loadable module an address-of-any-symbol lookup is a
substantial weakening on its own. Apply only to a disposable VM.
---
 kernel/kallsyms.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/kernel/kallsyms.c b/kernel/kallsyms.c
index a9a0ca6..2d1f3d1 100644
--- a/kernel/kallsyms.c
+++ b/kernel/kallsyms.c
@@ -242,6 +242,7 @@ unsigned long kallsyms_lookup_name(const char *name)
 
 	return module_kallsyms_lookup_name(name);
 }
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(kallsyms_lookup_name);
 
 /*
  * Iterate over all symbols in vmlinux.  For symbols from modules use
